  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThis afternoon to Thursday: Widespread Rainfall\n\nLight rain showers are moving east across Iowa and Minnesota at\nthis time. This activity will reach the western portions of the\nforecast area here shortly and should continue to move east \nover the region the rest of today. This activity is forming in \nan axis of increased mid level moisture and it has synoptic \nsupport with PVA associated with shortwave troughs. This first \ninitial round of rainfall likely will not be all that heavy, \nsince instability will be very low today and PWAT values are \nstill rising. Still, some areas may receive upwards of 0.50-0.75\ninches of rain before sunrise tomorrow morning. As this first \nround of rain ends from west to east tonight, fog may form in \nits wake due to the increased surface moisture. The latest hi- \nres ensemble output has the higher potential for fog across \ncentral Minnesota, however there are members that bring the bank\nof fog into western Wisconsin into southeastern Minnesota. Any \nfog that does develop should dissipate a few hours after sunrise\non Wednesday morning.\n\nA break in the rainfall is likely for most of the forecast area on \nWednesday before the second and heavier round of rainfall forms \nduring the afternoon into Thursday morning. PWAT values will \ncontinue to climb into the 1.5-1.75 inch range with the potential \nfor the far southern counties to approach 2 inches based on mean \nensemble PWAT values. Model guidance is now finally starting to \nagree with the location for the heaviest rainfall and it has been \ntrending further south compared to 24 hours ago. The EC/EC AIFS have \nbeen indicating that this corridor of heavy rain would mainly set up \nin Iowa into southern Wisconsin for multiple days whereas other \nmodel guidance has had no consistency with the placement of the\nheaviest rainfall. A majority of guidance is now leaning \ntowards the southern solution and the trend may even continue to\npush the heaviest rain further south out of the CWA. If the \nheaviest rain still reaches our area, only the far southern \ncounties are expected to get this rainfall and it should hold \noff until Wednesday night into Thursday morning based on the \nlatest hi-res models. Rainfall amounts are still pretty \nuncertain, but the latest 29/13z NBM still indicates a 40-60% \nchance to reach or exceed 2 inches for Fayette county to Grant \ncounty. High end amounts from this system continues to be \nupwards of 3-4 inches so if those amounts are actually achieved,\nsome flooding or river flooding concerns may arise. Rain will \ncome to an end on Thursday from northwest to southeast as a cold\nfront ushers in a drier airmass.\n\nFriday into Early Next Week: Mostly Dry and Trending Cooler\n\nThe H300 jet stream is forecast to sit across southern Canada and \nmay dig south at times into the Great Lakes region to northeastern \nUS this weekend into early next week. With a cold front south of the \nlocal forecast area and some cooler air filtering in due to the \ntroughing, daily high temperatures will be a few degrees cooler into \nearly next week. The cooler air mass will be more noticeable at\nnight with lows dropping into the 40s to 30s. As of now, Monday\nmorning looks to be coolest morning over the next week with the\ncoldest temperatures potentially approaching freezing across \ncentral Wisconsin. Mostly dry conditions are expected during \nthis time frame with the exception being on Saturday as there \nare low chances for light rain showers over the northern half of\nthe CWA.",
